<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1848</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo, signed on 2 February 1848, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> as the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and ceded Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, about half of its territory, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in return for 15 million dollars and the settlement of American claims. Mexicans in the ceded lands were promised property rights and citizenship. The loss shaped Mexican politics for decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo.jpg" alt="Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governments of USA and Mexico · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TreatyOfGuadalupeHidalgoCover.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
